    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Sequential electronic reconstruction enables durable oxygen redox in water electrolysis","description":"Electrocatalytic water oxidation is central to green hydrogen production, but its efficiency is constrained by rigid scaling relationships governing conventional metal catalysts. Activating lattice-oxygen redox breaks these barriers, yet it often destabilizes the material, causing rapid degradation under demanding electrolysis conditions. Here we report a sequential reconstruction activation paradigm that enables sustained oxygen redox through electronically ordered lattice restructuring. Under anodic polarization, initial Se leaching from a Cr2O3/NiSe2 precursor broadens the \n              $${e}_{{\\rm{g}}}^{* }$$\n              \n             band and accelerates *OH deprotonation, strengthening metal–oxygen covalency. Subsequent Cr leaching selectively depletes Ni-co-ordinated oxygen, generating spatially confined oxygen non-bonding states. This sequential electronic reconstruction yields a modified NiOOH phase that promotes lattice-oxygen participation while suppressing destructive redox delocalization, thereby establishing a durable oxygen-redox window at high current densities. Consequently, the catalyst enables anion-exchange-membrane water electrolysis at 4.1 A cm−2 at 1.8 V in 1 M KOH at 80 °C and sustains stable operation for nearly 3,500 h under accelerated ageing at 60 °C. These findings establish sequential electronic reconstruction as a strategy to decouple oxygen-redox activity from lattice instability, providing a design framework for durable, high-rate catalysts for water electrolysis. Electrocatalytic water oxidation remains a bottleneck for green hydrogen production due to efficiency limits. Here the authors show a sequential reconstruction activation strategy that overcomes these issues by decoupling oxygen-redox activity from lattice instability, enabling exciting performance.","datePublished":"2026-09-22T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-09-22T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"1","pageEnd":"12","sameAs":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41893-026-01940-6","keywords":["Electrocatalysis","Hydrogen energy","Sustainable Development"],"image":["https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41893-026-01940-6/MediaObjects/41893_2026_1940_Fig1_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41893-026-01940-6/MediaObjects/41893_2026_1940_Fig2_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41893-026-01940-6/MediaObjects/41893_2026_1940_Fig3_HTML.png","https://media.springernature.com/lw1200/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41893-026-01940-6/MediaObjects/41893_2026_1940_Fig4_HTML.png"],"isPartOf":{"name":"Nature Sustainability","issn":["2398-9629"],"@type":["Periodical"]},"publisher":{"name":"Nature Publishing Group UK","logo":{"url":"https://www.springernature.com/app-sn/public/images/logo-springernature.png","@type":"ImageObject"},"@type":"Organization"},"author":[{"name":"Junchen Yu","url":"http://orcid.org/0009-0001-7751-8348","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore","address":{"name":"Department of Materials Science and Engineering, National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"@type":"Person"},{"name":"Haoyin Zhong","url":"http://orcid.org/0000-0002-3838-6872","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore","address":{"name":"Department of Materials Science and Engineering, National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore","@type":"PostalAddress"},"@type":"Organization"}],"email":"stbzzhy@nus.edu.sg","@type":"Person"},{"name":"Qi Zhang","url":"http://orcid.org/0000-0001-9540-7791","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore","address":{"name":"Department of Materials Science and Engineering, National University of Singapore, Singapore,