    <meta name="description" content="Electrocatalytic water oxidation is central to green hydrogen production, but its efficiency is constrained by rigid scaling relationships governing conventional metal catalysts. Activating lattice-oxygen redox breaks these barriers, yet it often destabilizes the material, causing rapid degradation under demanding electrolysis conditions. Here we report a sequential reconstruction activation paradigm that enables sustained oxygen redox through electronically ordered lattice restructuring. Under anodic polarization, initial Se leaching from a Cr2O3/NiSe2 precursor broadens the $${e}_{{\rm{g}}}^{* }$$ band and accelerates *OH deprotonation, strengthening metal&#8211;oxygen covalency. Subsequent Cr leaching selectively depletes Ni-co-ordinated oxygen, generating spatially confined oxygen non-bonding states. This sequential electronic reconstruction yields a modified NiOOH phase that promotes lattice-oxygen participation while suppressing destructive redox delocalization, thereby establishing a durable oxygen-redox window at high current densities. Consequently, the catalyst enables anion-exchange-membrane water electrolysis at 4.1&#8201;A&#8201;cm&#8722;2 at 1.8&#8201;V in 1&#8201;M KOH at 80&#8201;&#176;C and sustains stable operation for nearly 3,500&#8201;h under accelerated ageing at 60&#8201;&#176;C. These findings establish sequential electronic reconstruction as a strategy to decouple oxygen-redox activity from lattice instability, providing a design framework for durable, high-rate catalysts for water electrolysis. Electrocatalytic water oxidation remains a bottleneck for green hydrogen production due to efficiency limits. Here the authors show a sequential reconstruction activation strategy that overcomes these issues by decoupling oxygen-redox activity from lattice instability, enabling exciting performance."/>
