                </div>

                <div class="c-article-body">
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Electrocatalytic water oxidation is central to green hydrogen production, but its efficiency is constrained by rigid scaling relationships governing conventional metal catalysts. Activating lattice-oxygen redox breaks these barriers, yet it often destabilizes the material, causing rapid degradation under demanding electrolysis conditions. Here we report a sequential reconstruction activation paradigm that enables sustained oxygen redox through electronically ordered lattice restructuring. Under anodic polarization, initial Se leaching from a Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>/NiSe<sub>2</sub> precursor broadens the <span class="mathjax-tex">\({e}_{{\rm{g}}}^{* }\)</span> band and accelerates <sup>*</sup>OH deprotonation, strengthening metal–oxygen covalency. Subsequent Cr leaching selectively depletes Ni-co-ordinated oxygen, generating spatially confined oxygen non-bonding states. This sequential electronic reconstruction yields a modified NiOOH phase that promotes lattice-oxygen participation while suppressing destructive redox delocalization, thereby establishing a durable oxygen-redox window at high current densities. Consequently, the catalyst enables anion-exchange-membrane water electrolysis at 4.1 A cm<sup>−2</sup> at 1.8 V in 1 M KOH at 80 °C and sustains stable operation for nearly 3,500 h under accelerated ageing at 60 °C. These findings establish sequential electronic reconstruction as a strategy to decouple oxygen-redox activity from lattice instability, providing a design framework for durable, high-rate catalysts for water electrolysis.</p></div></div></section>
                    
    


                    
                        

                        
